                    "text": "param output_gain_db 0 @min -24 @max 36",
                    "varname": "output_gain_db"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-38",
                    "linecount": 71,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1043.0 ],
                    "text": "================================================================================\nEFFECT: comp_fairchild_6386_varimu_mono\nSOURCE: Raffensperger (DAFx-2012, \"Toward a Wave Digital Filter Model of the\n        Fairchild 670 Limiter\", Sections 2, 5, 6; Eqs. 1, 10-14; Fig. 8; Table 3)\nTYPE:   Mono Variable-Mu 6386 Remote-Cutoff Triode Feedback Limiter with\n        3-Capacitor Program-Dependent Memory\n================================================================================\n\nOVERVIEW\n--------\nMono (single-channel) version of comp_fairchild_6386_varimu. Same DSP model, but\nwith one sidechain and one 6386 push-pull stage instead of two, and no\nLateral/Vertical (mid/side) matrix — so the mono feed cannot drift in level\nbetween the two channels of the stereo version, and CPU cost is roughly halved.\n\nModels the 1950s Fairchild 670 variable-mu vacuum-tube feedback limiter.\nIts sidechain monitors the output signal through a Class-B crossover stage with\ninteracting AC and DC threshold controls (Eq. 10), rectified into a three-capacitor\nRC network (CT fast, CU medium, CV slow memory, Fig. 8 & Table 3) that elongates\nrelease time after sustained heavy peaks. The signal path models a balanced\npush-pull pair of GE 6386 remote-cutoff triodes whose odd-order harmonic\nsaturation increases proportionally with gain reduction (Sec. 6, Fig. 13).\n\nIO\n--\nin1 is the only audio input; the processed mono signal is written to both out1\nand out2 (mono-to-both), so it can be dropped into a stereo chain without\ncollapsing one side. in3 (pitch Hz) and in4 (mod Hz) are unused.\n\nPARAMETERS (8 total)\n--------------------\n1. input_gain_db   [-24.0 .. +36.0 dB, default: 0.0]\n   Input trim, a plain gain stage ahead of the input transformer. Raising it\n   feeds more level into the existing limiter (so it limits harder, as on real\n   hardware); it does not alter any other setting.\n\n2. drive_db        [0.0 .. 24.0 dB, default: 8.0]\n   Input step attenuator / transformer drive into the 6386 variable-mu stage.\n\n3. ac_thresh       [0.0 .. 1.0, default: 0.55]\n   Front-panel AC Threshold (phi_AC): controls sidechain sensitivity and amount\n   of feedback limiting.\n\n4. dc_knee         [0.0 .. 1.0, default: 0.25]\n   Under-chassis DC Threshold (phi_DC, Eq. 10): biases the Class-B sidechain\n   crossover stage, continuously morphing from soft vari-mu compression to hard\n   brickwall limiting.\n\n5. tc_mode         [1.0 .. 6.0, default: 5.0]\n   Six-way Fairchild Time-Constant selector (Table 3):\n   - 1..4: Single-RC release (100 ms, 300 ms, 880 ms, 1.76 s).\n   - 5: Dual-RC program-dependent release (CT + CU).\n   - 6: Triple-RC dual-memory auto-release (CT + CU + CV) for fast transient\n        recovery and slow sustained leveling.\n\n6. tube_harmonics  [0.0 .. 1.0, default: 0.5]\n   Gain-reduction-dependent push-pull 6386 3rd-harmonic saturation depth\n   (Raffensperger Sec. 6, Fig. 13).\n\n7. mix             [0.0 .. 1.0, default: 1.0]\n   Dry/wet parallel NY-compression blend.\n\n8. output_gain_db  [-24.0 .. +36.0 dB, default: 0.0]\n   Output trim, a plain gain stage after the feedback tap and the dry/wet\n   blend, so it makes the effect up or down in level without feeding back into\n   the sidechain.\n\nNOTE\n----\nRemoved versus the stereo original: lat_vert_mode (Left/Right vs Lateral/Vertical\nmatrix blend), which has no meaning with a single channel.\n",
                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                }
            }
        ],
